You're misreading what zero-cost means — and your result actually *confirms* the claim.

## "Zero-cost" means "same as handwritten", not "faster"

Stroustrup's original formulation: *what you don't use, you don't pay for; what you do use, you couldn't hand-code better*. The iterator chain compiles to essentially the same machine code as your old imperative loop. Identical speed is the **success case**. If iterators were faster than your loop, that would mean your imperative code was bad, not that iterators are magic.

So: nothing is wrong, nothing is oversold. But if you want this function to actually be *faster*, the bottleneck is elsewhere.

## The real limiter: `filter` blocks vectorization

A data-dependent branch (`x % 2 == 0`) inside the loop makes it hard for LLVM to auto-vectorize. Whether you write it as `filter` or `if` doesn't matter — the branch is the problem. Make it branchless:

```rust
fn sum_squared_evens_branchless(xs: &[i64]) -> i64 {
    xs.iter()
        .map(|&x| {
            let mask = ((x & 1) - 1); // 0 if odd... careful: -(x & 1 ^ 1)? Let's be explicit:
            let keep = -((x & 1 ^ 1)); // -1 (all ones) if even, 0 if odd
            (x * x) & keep
        })
        .sum()
}
```

Or more readably, let the compiler pick the select:

```rust
fn sum_squared_evens_select(xs: &[i64]) -> i64 {
    xs.iter()
        .map(|&x| if x % 2 == 0 { x * x } else { 0 })
        .sum()
}
```

This `if/else` is a *value selection*, not control flow — LLVM lowers it to a `select`/blend and can vectorize the whole loop (multiply, mask, horizontal add). The `filter` version has actual control flow, which kills that.

Check the asm difference on godbolt with `-C opt-level=3 -C target-cpu=x86-64-v3`: the `select` version should show `vpmullq`/`vpand`/`vpaddq` over packed lanes; the `filter` version shows a scalar loop with a conditional jump.

## Caveat on the multiply

`i64 * i64` vectorization is weak before AVX-512 (`vpmullq` is AVX-512DQ). On default `x86-64` target LLVM may emit a clunky 64-bit multiply emulation; benchmark with your actual `target-cpu`. If your values fit in `i32`, narrowing the element type often gives a bigger win than anything else here — twice the lanes and cheap multiplies.

## Measure properly

```rust
use criterion::{black_box, criterion_group, criterion_main, Criterion};

fn bench(c: &mut Criterion) {
    let xs: Vec<i64> = (0..1_000_000).collect();
    c.bench_function("filter", |b| b.iter(|| sum_squared_evens(black_box(&xs))));
    c.bench_function("select", |b| b.iter(|| sum_squared_evens_select(black_box(&xs))));
}
criterion_group!(benches, bench);
criterion_main!(benches);
```

On typical AVX2/AVX-512 hardware expect the `select` version to be 2–6x faster on a 1M-element slice (until you go memory-bound — at 8 MB you're past L2, so the ceiling may become DRAM bandwidth, ~not~ ALU throughput).

**TL;DR:** iterators matching imperative code is zero-cost abstraction *working*. Your perf headroom is in vectorization, and the fix is removing the data-dependent branch, not changing the abstraction style.

# The Component Model, Minus the Spec-Speak

The mental model: **a core wasm module is like a `.o` file** — it only speaks integers and floats, and shares one big linear memory. **A component is like a shared library with a real ABI** — it has typed interfaces (strings, lists, records, results), its own encapsulated memory, and can be linked against other components regardless of source language.

The thing that makes this work is **WIT** (WebAssembly Interface Types... the language, not the dead 2019 proposal — more on that below). You write one `.wit` file, and each language's tooling generates bindings from it.

## 1. Define the shared interface

```wit
// wit/plugin.wit
package my-org:[EMAIL];

interface text-processor {
    record doc {
        title: string,
        body: string,
    }

    variant process-error {
        too-large(u64),
        invalid-encoding,
        other(string),
    }

    process: func(input: doc) -> result<doc, process-error>;
    name: func() -> string;
}

world plugin {
    export text-processor;
}
```

Key syntax notes:

- `package` namespaces things; versions are real and tooling checks them.
- `record` = struct, `variant` = tagged union (Rust enum), and you also get `enum`, `flags`, `option<T>`, `result<T, E>`, `list<T>`, `tuple<...>`, and `resource` (handle-based objects with methods — what you'd use for stateful plugin instances).
- A **`world`** is the contract for a whole component: what it `export`s (implements) and what it `import`s (needs from the host). Plugins export; your host imports.

## 2. Implement in Rust

```bash
cargo add wit-bindgen
cargo component new --lib my-plugin   # or wire it up manually
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({ world: "plugin" });

use exports::my_org::plugins::text_processor::{Guest, Doc, ProcessError};

struct MyPlugin;

impl Guest for MyPlugin {
    fn process(input: Doc) -> Result<Doc, ProcessError> {
        if input.body.len() > 1_000_000 {
            return Err(ProcessError::TooLarge(input.body.len() as u64));
        }
        Ok(Doc { title: input.title.to_uppercase(), body: input.body })
    }
    fn name() -> String { "rust-upper".into() }
}

export!(MyPlugin);
```

```bash
cargo component build --release
# → target/wasm32-wasip1/release/my_plugin.wasm  (a *component*, not a module)
```

Notice you wrote `String` and `Result` — `wit-bindgen` generated all the marshaling. No `extern "C"`, no pointer/length pairs.

## 3. Implement in Python

```bash
pip install componentize-py
componentize-py -d wit -w plugin bindings .   # generates typed stubs
```

```python
# app.py
from plugin import exports
from plugin.exports.text_processor import Doc
from plugin.types import Err

class TextProcessor(exports.TextProcessor):
    def process(self, input: Doc) -> Doc:
        if len(input.body) > 1_000_000:
            raise Err(ProcessError_TooLarge(len(input.body)))
        return Doc(title=input.title, body=input.body[::-1])

    def name(self) -> str:
        return "py-reverser"
```

```bash
componentize-py -d wit -w plugin componentize app -o py_plugin.wasm
```

This bundles a Python interpreter into the component (~tens of MB), but the result is a self-contained `.wasm` that satisfies the *exact same world* as the Rust one.

(For JS, the equivalent is `jco componentize`, which embeds SpiderMonkey via StarlingMonkey.)

## 4. Run them from a host

With Rust + Wasmtime, the host side mirrors the guest:

```rust
wasmtime::component::bindgen!({ world: "plugin", path: "wit" });

let component = Component::from_file(&engine, "py_plugin.wasm")?;
let plugin = Plugin::instantiate(&mut store, &component, &linker)?;
let out = plugin.my_org_plugins_text_processor().call_process(&mut store, &doc)?;
```

The host code doesn't know or care which language produced the component. Same for the **plugins calling each other**: have one plugin's world `import` an interface another exports, then compose them with `wac` (`wac plug` or a composition file) — wiring happens at the WIT level, fully type-checked, before anything runs.

## Why this beats the old approaches

You may have seen the older "Interface Types" proposal — it died, and the component model is its successor. Practical differences:

1. **Memory isolation.** With raw modules, polyglot interop meant agreeing on memory layout and copying bytes between linear memories by hand. Components never share memory; the *canonical ABI* does lifting/lowering automatically.
2. **Real types at the boundary.** Old-style FFI gave you `(i32, i32)` for a string. WIT gives you `result<list<doc>, error>` and every language's bindgen renders it idiomatically.
3. **Composition without a host in the loop.** `wac` links components into a new component statically. With modules you'd write glue code in the host for every pairing.
4. **Versioned, inspectable contracts.** `wasm-tools component wit my_plugin.wasm` dumps the interface of any component — your plugin registry can validate compatibility mechanically.

## Gotchas to know up front

- Calls across components **copy** data (strings, lists). Don't design chatty interfaces passing megabytes per call; pass handles (`resource`) for big stateful things.
- Python/JS components are large because they embed a runtime. Fine for plugins, surprising the first time.
- Tooling moves fast; pin versions of `cargo-component`, `componentize-py`, `jco`, `wasm-tools`, and `wasmtime` together.
- Async/streams (WASI 0.3) is still landing — if plugins need streaming I/O, check current status before designing around it.

Start small: that one WIT file, the Rust and Python implementations above, and a 30-line Wasmtime host. Once you see the same host call both, the rest of the spec reads a lot less like glaze-inducing fog.

